#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""moat-scan-build - static review of a Rust, Python or Go project before its
build and install steps run.

Part of omarchy-moat 0.1.0.  Standard library only, no dependencies, no
network, no execution of anything it reads.

Third companion to moat-scan-pkgbuild and moat-scan-npm: same finding shape,
same allow file, same exit codes, same "here is how to proceed" posture.  It
answers the one question the other two answer, for the three ecosystems they
do not cover:

    if I let cargo / pip / go run right now, what would execute?

The rule for what is checked is exactly that - *where does this ecosystem run
code before the user has run anything?*

  cargo   build.rs and [build-dependencies] compile and run on `cargo build`;
          proc-macro crates run inside rustc; .cargo/config.toml decides which
          registry every crate comes from and which program is invoked as the
          linker, the compiler wrapper and the test runner.
  python  setup.py is executed by pip; a PEP 517 backend-path makes pip import
          code out of the package being installed; a *.pth file's `import`
          line runs on every interpreter start forever after.
  go      almost nothing: `go build` compiles a dependency, it never runs one.
          What is left is `go generate` (commands embedded in source), module
          `replace` (the code you compile is not the code go.mod names) and
          //go:linkname (binding to private runtime symbols).

One executable, three ecosystems, because the infrastructure (finding shape,
allow file, exit codes, rendering) and most of the detection vocabulary
(curl-pipe-shell, suspicious hosts, credential paths, base64/eval obfuscation,
invisible Unicode) are shared.  Three separate files would have been three
copies of it.  It is installed under three names as well - moat-scan-cargo,
moat-scan-pip and moat-scan-go - so each shim calls the tool for its own
ecosystem and the MOAT_SCANNER_* override is per ecosystem; argv[0] picks the
default, `--for` overrides it.

Exit codes: 0 = nothing (or only low), 1 = medium findings, 2 = high findings.
"""

